WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.999
Welcome, everybody, we are we're getting close,
very, very close to the election, and we predicted

00:00:05.999 --> 00:00:07.410
this time it could happen.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:13.000
You don't have to be a master of statistics
or mathematics to look at the corona virus

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.980
numbers and understand that we could be easily
heading towards hundred thousand cases a day

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.510
within several weeks.

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:26.760
And when Dr. Foushee said it months ago, when
Foushee said, if we don't do X, Y and Z, we

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:33.739
could be looking at 100000 American corona
virus cases per day in the fall.

00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:35.730
Some people said he sounded crazy.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.140
Some people said it made no sense.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.670
Some people said, oh, down, Fouche.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.399
She wants everything closed forever.

00:00:41.399 --> 00:00:45.420
But indeed, when you look at the numbers,
it appears that we are going in a direction

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.699
where part of the country is pretending as
though the pandemic is over.

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:53.999
They're behaving as though it's over.

00:00:53.999 --> 00:01:01.350
But we are set seeing new record numbers going
back several months in terms of cases.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.970
Of course, you have people saying, well, cases
are up, but deaths aren't up.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:11.440
And that is true sort of so far, although
if you look at the seven day average, we now

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.270
are seeing an uptick in the number of deaths.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.090
So let's sort of look at everything from the
beginning.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.130
Reset the pandemic so we can all get on the
same page.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:23.670
What I am going to put on the screen for you
here, and I will describe it if you're just

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:28.930
listening, is a chart of U.S. cases per day
dating all the way back to February.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:33.570
And then the blue line that you see, that's
the seven day moving average, which shows

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.000
us the general week to week direction of cases.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Are they generally going up or down?

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.810
So you'll see that in mid-April we peaked
with about 32000 cases a day.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:48.770
Health measures were able to reduce that down
to about 20000 cases a day in late May.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:50.840
But then things started opening up.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.830
And Dr. Foushee said, you know, we're not
really at the place where we can do this.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:57.430
We're only down to 20000 cases a day.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:02.390
We need to be down at least a 10000 a day
with at least a million tests a day to reopen.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:07.920
People didn't listen, so we got another spike
and we went back up not to 32000 cases a day,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.489
but all the way up to 70000 cases a day in
mid-July.

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:17.260
And then again, with people being mostly outdoors
and lots of places taking more serious measures.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:23.790
We were able to get back down to 35000 cases
a day in early September.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.159
So the first time after a spike, we got down
to 20 K.

00:02:28.159 --> 00:02:33.810
The next spike was bigger and we only got
down to 35 K, and then we reopened more stuff.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.680
Movie theaters, casinos.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:41.879
And now in the last few days, we've had some
of the worst days, including 72000 cases in

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:44.010
one day on Friday.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:49.140
And when you look at the trend line now, you
see that we are headed up and potentially

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.670
to 100000 cases in a single day within a few
weeks.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.939
Now, there are folks who are going to say,
but but.

00:02:55.939 --> 00:02:56.939
But, but, David.

00:02:56.939 --> 00:02:58.650
Deaths aren't going up.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:03.099
Deaths lag cases at by at least two to three
weeks.

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:07.849
We now are better at treating this and better
at keeping people alive longer.

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:12.170
So it would extend that lag time even a little
bit more.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:18.200
But if you look at the trend line on deaths,
you will see that after a seven week decline

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.159
in the death trend line, it leveled out in
early September.

00:03:22.159 --> 00:03:27.269
And in the last few days, there's a small,
but it is there blip upwards on deaths.

00:03:27.269 --> 00:03:29.999
And the expectation is that it will continue
to climb.

00:03:29.999 --> 00:03:35.480
Now, I am the first to admit that there is
some good news here, which is that we are

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.409
better at treating this now than we were six
months ago.

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:44.329
I expect that given X number of cases today
versus X number of cases six months ago, fewer

00:03:44.329 --> 00:03:45.510
people would die today.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.580
Now in part, that's because younger people
on average are getting the disease right now.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:55.340
In part, it's because you have very few, if
any, hospitals overwhelmed right now.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.200
And in part, it's because we have better treatment
protocol and some medications that are helping

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.069
some of the sickest patients as soon as hospitals
get overwhelmed.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:07.989
If it happens, which they might, as we enter
flu season, that could change.

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:11.730
And you could see the rate of death go up
as well.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:12.730
Hopefully it won't.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.980
Now, similar things are happening in other
countries.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:19.829
You remember Italy was one of the early hot
spots brutally hit.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:21.840
They got it under control.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.240
They have now had their worst week in the
entire pandemic in terms of cases in the last

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:27.240
week.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.360
They had a record almost 12000 cases yesterday.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.590
So Italy is spiking.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:37.349
One other example, France, which had it very
much under control, also with record spikes

00:04:37.349 --> 00:04:39.240
over the last 10 days.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.770
Much worse than what they had in March and
April.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:46.010
And remember, Italy and Europe in general
have not yet had a second spike.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:50.341
If you look at our numbers, we had the April
spike and the July spike.

00:04:50.341 --> 00:04:53.830
And now we're seeing spike number three.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:58.860
Italy, France, European countries avoided
that second spike all summer.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:05.360
We may be heading to something unconscionably
worse than what Europe is heading towards.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.680
And meanwhile, Donald, Donald Trump continues
to hold huge rallies.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:14.590
So we'll talk on the bonus show later this
week about what fall and winter might look

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.180
like individually.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:21.129
I've been reading some medical recommendations
about Thanksgiving and sort of dealing with

00:05:21.129 --> 00:05:22.650
some realities there.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:26.970
Let me know what you are planning for, whether
you're in the United States or not for the

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.250
next four months, essentially.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:30.610
And we'll discuss that on an upcoming bonus
show.

